  "_style": "Write standard, warm, natural isiZulu (not overly formal, not slangy). Always address the user in the singular \"wena\" (u-/ku-/-kho: \"Ubhalele umlingani wakho\"); address the couple in the plural \"nina\" (ni-/-nu: \"Nobabili\", \"isihlahla senu\") and refer to them as \"nobabili\" or \"ndawonye\"; the app speaks of itself sparingly. The other person is always \"umlingani wakho\" (never isoka/intombi/umyeni/unkosikazi); Zulu has no grammatical gender, so keep pronouns as class-1 \"yena/u-/-khe\" and avoid gendered nouns entirely. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never prefixed or inflected (no \"ku-%@\", \"no-%@\", \"ka-%@\"): rephrase so the name stands alone, e.g. \"%@ ukuthumelele incwadi\" or \"Umlingani wakho (%@)\", and likewise never attach concords or prefixes to numbers or other placeholders. Use curly double quotes “ ” (inner ‘ ’), the single ellipsis character …, no space before ? ! : ;, keep emoji, \\n and the \" · \" separator exactly. Loanwords take a hyphenated vowel prefix (i-AI, i-akhawunti, i-Premium) but brand names stay as is (Coupley, CoupleSync, Premium). Numbers use Western digits; dates follow the system format (e.g. 14 Novemba 2026) and never get hand-written prefixes. Buttons and actions are short imperative verbs (Londoloza, Thumela, Zama futhi, Vala incwadi ngophawu); titles, tabs and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper names. Latin script only. Plural categories are [\"one\", \"other\"]: \"one\" uses the singular noun (\"usuku olu-%lld\" is avoided; prefer \"%lld usuku\" / \"%lld izinsuku\" with the number first). Tone: loving, light and kind, never cheesy or preachy.",
